WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Well into the eighties, corporate&nbsp;
CEOs were cigarette-smoking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.040
suit-wearing gentlemen who maybe&nbsp;
played some golf on the weekends.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.180
The CEOs of today are not&nbsp;
only rich they are ripped!

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:20.040
And I’m not just talking about Jeff Bezos who&nbsp;
famously went from looking like this…to this.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.940
I am talking about so many high-profile CEOs.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.320
Mark Zuckerberg

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.720
Telegram executive Pavel Durov

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.760
Record executive Scooter Braun

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.300
Businessman Strauss Zelnick&nbsp;
who posed in purple spandex.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.300
High-end real estate broker Jason Oppenheim.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.740
The Wall Street Journal calls it “the&nbsp;
latest power move for middle-aged men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.560
looking like an ad for protein powder.”

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.460
So, why is there suddenly&nbsp;
such a focus on getting fit?

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.700
Well, when selfies and social media are the norm,&nbsp;
there is a greater awareness of public image.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.660
Zuckerberg showed off his punches and kicks on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.900
Instagram as he trained with&nbsp;
a professional MMA fighter.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.800
He says he fell in love with mixed&nbsp;
martial arts during the pandemic.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.800
The shift to remote work meant&nbsp;
people suddenly found themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.620
locked indoors with a lot&nbsp;
more time on their hands.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.040
While that could also mean&nbsp;
putting on the pandemic pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.200
those with money and a team behind them took&nbsp;
advantage of the time to focus on fitness.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.520
But getting ripped is not only&nbsp;
the result of the pandemic.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.980
It’s a turning of the tables of sorts.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.420
Men used to be judged by their shoes and&nbsp;
their watch. So long as they were rich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.300
they could slack off when it came&nbsp;
to their physical appearance.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.820
But that’s not really true anymore, especially&nbsp;
when women began to wield financial success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.200
when the workplace doors started to open&nbsp;
for them in the sixties and seventies.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.460
Female executives have long known&nbsp;
the importance of appearance – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.200
that includes staying physically fit.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.440
And now, the beauty standard has fallen on men.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.220
It’s almost unacceptable to be fat and flabby.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.580
When Elon Musk soaked up the sun&nbsp;
on a friend’s yacht in the summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.760
he got a lot of body shaming&nbsp;
for the way he looked.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.620
Some fun in the Mykonos sun may have been a&nbsp;
rarity for the richest person in the world.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.660
His lack of a tan could mean he’s&nbsp;
hard at work inside Tesla’s factories.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:17.700
By the way, you may recall Musk took a&nbsp;
jab at Bill Gates by tweeting a photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.660
of the Microsoft founder side by&nbsp;
side with the pregnant man emoji.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.809
Musk acknowledged on the podcast Full&nbsp;
Send that he needs to exercise more.

00:02:25.809 --> 00:02:25.895
Musk acknowledged on the podcast Full Send&nbsp;
that he needs to exercise more even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:25.973
he doesn’t like it. He says he’ll try&nbsp;
to work out for at least 20 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.973 --> 00:02:26.040
as soon as he wakes up instead of&nbsp;
immediately looking at his phone.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.360
I gotta work out and be in better shape. So.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.620
We need you around. Thanks. I actually don’t&nbsp;
really like working out but I gotta do it.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.400
He says he’ll try to work out for at&nbsp;
least 20 minutes as soon as he wakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.800
up instead of immediately looking at his phone.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.000
There’s perhaps another deeper reason for CEOs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.660
go from looking like nerds to&nbsp;
looking like Thor these days.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:56.160
Exercise also releases brain&nbsp;
chemicals that improve your memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.680
concentration, and mental sharpness.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.500
Giving you a major advantage in the workplace.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.680
In the way pro athletes prepare for competition,&nbsp;
running a company is extremely competitive:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.320
mentally and physically demanding with long&nbsp;
days that stretch into nights and weekends.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:20.880
Musk famously worked 120 hours a week or 17 hours&nbsp;
a day - during his company’s most trying times.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.920
Some CEOs believe they wouldn’t be where&nbsp;
they are today without staying in shape.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.580
Despite his busy schedule running Virgin Group,&nbsp;
Richard Branson finds time to exercise every day.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.660
When I wake up in the morning, I normally&nbsp;
go and play a very hard game of tennis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.880
really early, about 6 in the morning with&nbsp;
somebody who’s a lot better than me. A pro.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.980
And then, I might go for a quick kite surf.&nbsp;
I live on an island so I’m lucky. I’ll then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:54.000
go into the gym with somebody, maybe&nbsp;
do 40 minutes of weightlifting one day.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.540
And then, the next day, I will&nbsp;
run or ride bikes or whatever.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.620
The 72-year-old swears by the&nbsp;
fact that fitness has been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.720
key to his success, saying in an interview:

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.680
“I definitely can achieve twice&nbsp;
as much in a day by keeping fit.”

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:17.100
Mark Cuban’s proudest exercise moment is&nbsp;
when he bench-pressed 300 pounds and also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.500
when the Dallas Mavericks owner dunked a&nbsp;
basketball for the first time at the age of 37.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.520
Apparently, a CEO’s fitness level&nbsp;
DOES affect the bottom line.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.940
In a decade-long study of 1,500 executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:36.060
the University of Cologne found in 2015&nbsp;
that a CEO’s physical fitness was associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:41.460
with higher firm profitability and higher&nbsp;
merger and acquisition announcement returns.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.860
So, a healthy leader makes the company more money&nbsp;
- for both themselves and for their shareholders.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:53.520
Allowing the wealthy CEO to splurge on yachts&nbsp;
- this one is reportedly Bezos’ – property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.520
and investing in their own health even more.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:02.700
Being healthy so you feel better and perform&nbsp;
your best should be top of mind for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:07.800
not only those with billions of dollars.
